## 1. MongoDB vs PostgreSQL: I'd choose PostgreSQL

For this application, PostgreSQL is my recommendation. Your data is highly structured: shops have owners, orders belong to shops, each order has print settings, and each shop has its own pricing rules.

| Consideration                    | PostgreSQL                          | MongoDB                                                           |
| -------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Shops, users, orders, pricing    | Excellent relational model          | Good                                                              |
| Data consistency                 | Strong transactions and constraints | Transactions supported, but relationships are application-managed |
| Order numbering                  | Transactions and unique constraints | Atomic counters and unique indexes                                |
| Price calculations and reporting | Excellent SQL support               | Aggregation pipelines                                             |
| Changing document metadata       | Supports JSONB                      | Naturally flexible documents                                      |
| Your application                 | My pick                             | Also viable                                                       |

PostgreSQL gives us foreign keys, unique constraints, transactions, and straightforward reporting. We can still store flexible metadata in JSONB when needed.

For example, the database can enforce that an order references a valid shop, while a unique constraint prevents duplicate order numbers within that shop.

One caveat: if you already have a reliable MongoDB instance deployed, migrating to PostgreSQL solely for this project may not be worth the operational overhead. But if we're starting fresh, I'd use PostgreSQL.

